{
  "case_id": "2017-16-961",
  "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2017-16-961",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cd79fd9f6a19-0000-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:04 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2017-16-961",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cd79fd9f6a19-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. VLADECK: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: At the heart of these complicated cases is the simple and well-settled distinction between federal officers who are appointed to a second office and those who have been assigned to exercise additional duties germane to their original appointment who hold one office, not two."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2017-16-961",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cd79fd9f6a19-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That's why when the four judges at issue here began to serve as appointed judges on the CMCR, the Court of Military Commission Review, it violated the dual office-holding ban and disqualified them from hearing the Petitioners' appeals."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2017-16-961",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cd79fd9f6a19-0000-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 13,
        "start": 13
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There are, of course, examples where Congress has expressly authorized military officers to hold a second, even civil office. And perhaps the most common and familiar example is the CIA director."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2017-16-961",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cd79fd9f6a19-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Mr. Vladeck, you didn't raise the amici's point, but what position do you take with respect to that point?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Bamzai",
      "case_id": "2017-16-961",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cd79fd9f6a19-0001-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BAMZAI: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, may it please the Court: This Court lacks Article III jurisdiction to issue the writs in these cases."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2017-16-961",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cd79fd9f6a19-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: May I go back to the beginning of your argument? You said that the CAAF exercises original jurisdiction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2017-16-961",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cd79fd9f6a19-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: But -- but that's all\nwith reference to the cases. \"The Supreme\nCourt shall have appellate jurisdiction\" over\nthese various cases. It doesn't talk about,\nyou know, which particular courts or whether\nsomebody's exercising which particular powers.\nIt just talks about cases."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Brian H. Fletcher (Respondent)",
      "case_id": "2017-16-961",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cd79fd9f6a19-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FLETCHER: Thank you, Mr. Chief\nJustice, and may it please the Court:\nBecause a question has been raised\nabout this Court's jurisdiction, I'd like to\nstart by explaining why, in the government's\nview, Section 1259 validly grants this Court\nappellate jurisdiction to review the Court of\nAppeals for the Armed Forces' decisions."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2017-16-961",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cd79fd9f6a19-0002-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "On the jurisdictional question, we\nstart, as I think all the parties before the\nCourt do, with Chief Justice Marshall's\ndecision for the Court in Marbury versus\nMadison, which says that the essential\ncriterion of appellate jurisdiction is that it\nrevises and corrects the proceedings in a cause\nalready instituted and does not institute that\ncause."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2017-16-961",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cd79fd9f6a19-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: -- with -- with no\nappeal and -- and -- and then it has a federal\nquestion and they come to us?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Brian H. Fletcher (Respondent)",
      "case_id": "2017-16-961",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cd79fd9f6a19-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "government that exercises the powers or\nauthorities of the civil government. It's a\nnon-military office.\nJudged by that criterion, a judgeship\non the Court of Military Commission Review is a\nmilitary office. As its name suggests, the\nCourt of Military Commission Review is a\nmilitary court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2017-16-961",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cd79fd9f6a19-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "to dispute that a judgeship on the court of criminal appeals is a military office, not a civil office of the sort that would be contemplated in Section 973."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2017-16-961",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cd79fd9f6a19-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 70,
        "start": 70
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the President did not create a problem, did not put those officers in an office that they're not authorized to hold by Congress. He just ratified their placement in that office through the means that Congress specified."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2017-16-961",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cd79fd9f6a19-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 71,
        "start": 71
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "we hope that the Court answers the question presented on the merits and holds that 973(b) does not prevent military officers from serving on the CMCR"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2017-16-961",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cd79fd9f6a19-0003-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 73,
        "start": 73
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: If your reading of the authorization or the savings clause is correct, then you really have no reason to fix any problem that's been identified, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2017-16-961",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cd79fd9f6a19-0003-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 76,
        "start": 76
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think it's important to stress the emptiness of the government's suggestion that it has been engaged in rigorous administrative enforcement of the statute."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2017-16-961",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "cd79fd9f6a19c62ebc53ccd8cfd3ad754e6b333843bcd9b86f8fe3bcd5df6a2f",
